{"name":"Guava","hex":"#ff982e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"guava","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tropical tree or shrub of the myrtle family, Psidium guajava.","Its yellowish tropical fruit, globular or pear-shaped with thin, yellow, green or brown skin, is often made into jams and jellies."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/guava"}],"entries":[{"title":"Guava","blocks":[{"text":"Guava, also known as the 'guava-pear' in various regions, is a common tropical fruit cultivated in many tropical and subtropical regions. The common guava Psidium guajava is a small tree in the myrtle family (Myrtaceae), native to Mexico, Central America, the Caribbean and northern South America. Botanically, guavas are berries."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Guava_pink_fruit.jpg/330px-Guava_pink_fruit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guava","wikidata":"Q3181909"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Guava_fruit_tree.jpg/500px-Guava_fruit_tree.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Guava_fruit_tree.jpg/500px-Guava_fruit_tree.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Guava fruit tree","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGuava_fruit_tree.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Guava_flowers_%286700015761%29.jpg/250px-Guava_flowers_%286700015761%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Guava_flowers_%286700015761%29.jpg/500px-Guava_flowers_%286700015761%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Guava